WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
The first film followed DNA sequence to proteins and traits.

2
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.340
Does more DNA have to mean a more complex organism?

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.160
To test that idea, we need counts from real chromosome sets, not guesses.

4
00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:22.003
DNA amount means total base pairs in a full chromosome set.

5
00:00:23.263 --> 00:00:25.423
A base pair is a ladder rung.

6
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:29.503
Scientists sequenced some living things and estimated others.

7
00:00:30.903 --> 00:00:34.103
These methods count DNA; they do not score complexity.

8
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:38.097
Watch each ladder become a counter, then a bar.

9
00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:41.837
Each bar shows published data for an organism.

10
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:46.697
Every estimated value keeps its estimate label on screen.

11
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.700
The rows include a bacterium and fungus, then plant and animal examples.

12
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.460
Every group represented here has DNA.

13
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.280
That supports a careful claim about this table.

14
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.380
It does not let this small set represent every living thing everywhere.

15
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:11.073
If amount ranked complexity, where should the human bar end compared with the onion

16
00:01:11.073 --> 00:01:15.073
bar? Make your prediction before either bar grows.

17
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:21.093
Remember, this test compares a full chromosome set with a full chromosome set.

18
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:31.570
The human bar grows, but the onion bar continues far beyond it.

19
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.670
The table's division appears on screen.

20
00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.510
The onion chromosome set has several times the human amount.

21
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:44.610
A larger DNA total does not rank organism complexity.

22
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:48.233
Now reveal every bar in data order.

23
00:01:49.753 --> 00:01:53.653
Rice extends beyond fruit fly, and onion extends beyond human.

24
00:01:54.893 --> 00:01:58.033
The pattern does not march from simpler to more complex.

25
00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:03.153
Total base count and organism complexity are different questions.

26
00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:08.997
Amount counts all base pairs, not only genes carrying protein instructions.

27
00:02:10.337 --> 00:02:12.837
It also does not tell which genes a cell uses.

28
00:02:14.577 --> 00:02:16.517
Sequence and gene use help explain traits.

29
00:02:17.757 --> 00:02:20.237
A total length cannot replace that information.

30
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.940
Now both halves meet.

31
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:29.720
DNA sequence helps shape traits; DNA amount does not rank organism complexity.

32
00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:32.220
The rule has scope.

33
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.360
Amount here means base pairs in one chromosome set.

34
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:43.100
Sequence helps through genes, proteins and gene use, and many traits also depend on

35
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.100
the surroundings.

36
00:02:44.833 --> 00:02:48.733
These bars come from published lab studies, not field samples.

37
00:02:50.193 --> 00:02:52.473
Most rows come from one strain or sample.

38
00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:55.253
Several values are estimates.

39
00:02:56.573 --> 00:03:00.973
The human row is a set without Y, and onion uses an earlier estimate.

40
00:03:02.393 --> 00:03:04.093
Good evidence keeps limits attached.

41
00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:09.247
The films began with a strawberry and person sharing DNA parts.

42
00:03:10.507 --> 00:03:12.387
The answer is not a bigger ladder.

43
00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:17.467
Different base orders and different gene use can guide different proteins.

44
00:03:19.027 --> 00:03:21.787
DNA amount does not rank organism complexity.
